      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "<div class=\"matrix-operands\">\n<div>$P=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 7 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -5 \\\\ -2 & -9 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -6 & -9 \\\\ 9 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$R=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$S=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -7 & 1 & 4 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the matrices $P,R,$ and $S?$\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that $P$ is a $5\\times 3$ matrix, $R$ is a $1\\times 3$ row vector, and $S$ is a $1\\times 5$ row vector. \n\nThere are $3$ columns in $R$ and $1$ row in $S.$ Since the number of columns in $R$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $S,$ the matrices $R$ and $S$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{R}_{1\\times 3}{\\times }_{\\ne }{S}_{1\\times 5}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $5$ columns in $S$ and $5$ rows in $P.$ Since the number of columns in $S$ equals the number of rows in $P,$ the matrices $S$ and $P$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{S}_{1\\times \\boxed{\\text{5}}}{\\times }_{\u2713}{P}_{\\boxed{\\text{5}}\\times 3}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $3$ columns in $R$ and $5$ rows in $P.$ Since the number of columns in $R$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $P,$ the matrices $R$ and $P$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{R}_{1\\times 3}{\\times }_{\\ne }{P}_{5\\times 3}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nThe product $SP$ is well defined. The products $RS$ and $RP$ are undefined.",
