    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "6d267664-d17a-51a3-aed2-8771e45a86c4",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "<div class=\"matrix-operands\">\n<div>$O=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 5 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3 & -7 & -4 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 9 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$P=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -9 & -7 & 7 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$R=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -5 & -6 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 7 & 9 \\\\ -9 & -9 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the matrices $O,P,$ and $R?$\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that $O$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix, $P$ is a $1\\times 5$ row vector, and $R$ is a $4\\times 3$ matrix.\n\nThere are $5$ columns in $P$ and $3$ rows in $O.$ Since the number of columns in $P$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $O,$ the matrices $P$ and $O$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{P}_{1\\times 5}{\\times }_{\\ne }{O}_{3\\times 4}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $3$ columns in $R$ and $1$ row in $P.$ Since the number of columns in $R$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $P,$ the matrices $R$ and $P$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{R}_{4\\times 3}{\\times }_{\\ne }{P}_{1\\times 5}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $3$ columns in $R$ and $3$ rows in $O.$ Since the number of columns in $R$ equals the number of rows in $O,$ the matrices $R$ and $O$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{R}_{4\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\times }_{\u2713}{O}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 4}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nThe product $RO$ is well defined. The products $PO$ and $RP$ are undefined.",
